# drain-sink acknowledgement allowlist (T-2531; moved to a tracked path by T-2681)
#
# Signature format: <relpath>::<enclosing-fn>::<sink>
# `#`-comments and blank lines are skipped. fn-name-based, so entries survive line
# moves; a fn RENAME re-fires the site — intended re-review on meaningful change.
#
# WHY THIS FILE IS GIT-TRACKED: see .context/checks/alloc-sink-allowlist's header
# and T-2681. Short version: it used to live under gitignored `.context/working/`,
# so all six sites below fired in any fresh clone / CI runner / worktree while
# CLAUDE.md documented the tree as "scans CLEAN".
#
# ALL SIX ARE THE SAME SHAPE, and each was re-verified for T-2681: the drained
# child is the SELF binary (`Command::new(&std::env::current_exe())`) run on a
# fixed, trusted subcommand — or `bash <script>` from the resolved scripts dir —
# with `kill_on_drop(true)`, `stdin(Stdio::null())`, and a
# `tokio::time::timeout(...)` wrapper. The captured output is therefore bounded by
# our own subcommand's JSON emission, not by peer-supplied bytes, which puts them
# outside the T-2529 threat model (peer-driven accumulation into the daemon).
#
# If any of these is ever changed to run a NON-self binary, or to drop the
# timeout / kill_on_drop, delete its line here — the acknowledgement no longer holds.

